# A scraper's own output is large, machine-specific and stale by the time
# anyone reads it. The two committed samples and the generated fixtures are
# the exception.
*.json
*.csv
!sample_output.json
!sample_output.csv
!fixtures_generated.json
screener_results.*
*.meta.json
*_debug.html
*_debug.png
*.page[0-9]
*.page[0-9][0-9]
*.page[0-9][0-9][0-9]

# ANY working directory a live run writes into, not one chosen name: a
# sibling committed a 1.5 MB raw dump through `live/` because only
# `live_results/` was listed. The KIND of directory is what is ignored.
live/
live_results/
out/
output/
run/
runs/
results/
tmp/
scratch/
dumps/
# The multi-megabyte raw captures the fixtures are cut from. They carry the
# session that fetched them (csrf tokens) and are never committed; the
# scrubbed, trimmed cut of them is fixtures_generated.json.
captures/

__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.fingerprint_cache/
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/
.venv/
venv/
env/
.DS_Store
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/
*.log
*.key
secrets*

# Every spelling of a working .env, not just `.env`: a `.env.bak` or
# `.env.local` is exactly how a live key gets one `git add -A` away from a
# commit. The negation keeps the documented example tracked.
.env*
!.env.example

# The first version of this scraper, kept locally for reference only.
legacy/
